<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Turn Off the Lights, Please. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Turn Off the Lights, Please.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Write instructions for playing a simple classroom game. Interactive: Write instructions for peers about how to improve their writing products.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write simple instructions for a game (e.g., "First, create energy-saving ideas. Then, act them out and ask peers to guess them.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can write instructions to help peers improve their writing by explaining steps clearly (e.g., "Step 1: Add more details about conservation." "Step 2: Use adjectives like 'helpful' or 'important' to make your ideas clearer." "Step 3: Check for capital letters and punctuation.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can give step-by-step instructions (e.g., "Use natural sunlight to dry the clothes and use energy-efficient light bulbs.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a picture of a light switch and a faucet. Students brainstorm (orally first, then write down) simple actions they can do at home to save energy and water. E.g., "Turn off the lights," "Close the faucet." (2-3 minutes).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing two simple instructions on the board using imperatives and target vocabulary: "First, turn off the light when you leave the room. Second, recycle old plastic bottles." The teacher pronounces "turn off" (/t/ sound) and "recycle" (/re/ prefix) clearly. "Let's save energy! It's easier than you think."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we use to tell someone what to do?" (Expected answer: "Instructions" or "Imperatives").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words tell us to do something, like 'Turn off'?" (Expected answer: "Verbs" or "Action words").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short set of illustrated instructions (e.g., a poster or handout) titled "Our Energy-Saving Game." The instructions describe a simple classroom game where students identify energy-saving actions. Example text: "<b>How to Play 'Energy Detectives':</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Create energy-saving ideas (e.g., 'Use solar power!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Act out one idea. Do not speak!</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask peers to guess the action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The person who guesses correctly gets a point.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Remember: Saving energy is the most important thing!</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand simple written instructions for a game related to conservation, focusing on imperatives and key vocabulary (energy, save, recycle, turn off, solar power, bulbs).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected: "How to play a game" or "Saving energy game").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher asks: "True or False: You need to talk when you act out an idea?" (Expected: "False"). "True or False: Saving energy is important?" (Expected: "True").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing simple imperative sentences and using target vocabulary (e.g., shade, light, morning, night, water, electricity, bulbs, faucet, recycle, turn off, save, conserve, help, reduce, waste, switch, cool, easy, meaningful, bright, safe, easier, most) to give instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Sentence Reordering):</b> The teacher writes jumbled instructions on the board (e.g., "the / off / lights / turn" or "water / save / Let's"). Students rewrite them correctly in their notebooks. E.g., "Turn off the lights." "Let's save water." (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences to form conservation instructions using words from a word bank (e.g., "Use ____ light." "____ the faucet." "____ old paper."). Word bank: natural, close, recycle. They write the full sentences. (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction (Write a Tip):</b> In pairs, students write two simple instructions/tips for saving energy or water at home. They must use an imperative and at least one target vocabulary word from the list. E.g., "Switch off the TV at night." "Conserve water in the shower." They then exchange their tips with another pair and check if the instructions are clear. (5 minutes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product: Students will write simple step-by-step instructions for a new classroom game about conservation. This connects directly to the theme "Turn Off the Lights, Please." and scenario "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students imagine a simple game (e.g., "Conservation Bingo," "Energy Match," "Water Drop Race") that teaches about saving energy or water.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They write 3-5 clear, simple instructions for their game, using imperatives and at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., "First, draw a picture of a light bulb. Then, write 'turn off' next to it.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages the use of adjectives like "helpful" or "important" and sequencing words (First, Then, Next). They can write, "Saving water is easier than saving electricity sometimes." or "Using solar power is the most important for the environment."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence starters provided: "First,..." "Then,..." "Next,..." "Remember to..." "Let's..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will exchange their written game instructions with a partner for peer assessment. They will provide simple written feedback to help peers improve their writing products.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b> Students use a simple checklist to evaluate their partner's instructions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there at least 3 steps? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are the instructions clear? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there imperative verbs? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are 2+ vocabulary words from the theme used (e.g., light, water, save, recycle)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are capital letters and periods used correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Based on the checklist, students write one suggestion for improvement (e.g., "Step 1: Add more details about the game." "Step 2: Use an adjective like 'bright' for the light." "Step 3: Check for capital letters.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned about writing instructions today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you will remember to do when writing instructions?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write instructions for a game? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn from your partner's feedback about your instructions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students were able to use imperatives effectively in their instructions. The peer assessment activity helped them understand the importance of clear steps and proper grammar (capital letters, periods). Some struggled with incorporating comparative/superlative adjectives naturally; I need to provide more sentence frames for this in the next lesson's writing tasks. The "Energy Detectives" game model was helpful. I will ensure more explicit modeling of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off" when reviewing vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use the game instructions they wrote to teach and play their conservation games, summarizing the rules to peers. This will integrate their writing into a communicative, project-based activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>